    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_79c5e439a1c7760f5f8bfb06344cbef0228990b05091dfd48620bedadf321fa1",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The contour lemma's separation of endpoint, off-saddle, and local Gaussian contributions becomes operational only after specialization: the mean-square phase converts it into a saddle condition with distinct frequency regimes and an omitted main term when no stationary point exists. Later sections route different ranges accordingly—monotonicity controls the small transition range, the specialized lemma handles the remaining oscillatory range, and the second-saddle analysis uses stationary-point admissibility together with partial summation to control frequencies near and beyond the cutoff. This staged error architecture is what permits local estimates to be aggregated without imposing one false global approximation.",
