    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_8fafd049e4014aab69a6c3c14ede0237f10aea90ee4686e4a3a052682cfa38f7",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source obtains its two oscillatory contributions through different branches of one representation pipeline. Difference indexing and Poisson continuation first produce the critical off-diagonal function; the divisor-summatory split then decomposes its mean integral into I1-I2+I3-I4. Applying the specialized saddle estimate directly to I1 assembles the first finite oscillatory sum, whereas I4 must first expose the oscillatory divisor remainder, change variables to transformed integrals J_n, and pass through a second stationary-point selection to produce the complementary sum. The two sums therefore encode distinct routes through the decomposition rather than duplicate approximations to one term.",
